\tableofcontents

This page collects design philosophy notes, and practical tips that
help working with Peano and its extensions. Where the @ref page_architecture_home
"architecture page" explains how the code is organised into layers, this page captures
*why* recurring implementation choices were made, and *how* to work with the
code base day to day, so both can be reused (or deliberately deviated from) consistently.


# AI usage

A lot of Peano's conventions are now written down as skills, so they are ready to
use for AI. The same holds for basic, reoccuring design steps: Some developers obviously
will write down steps directly in Python or modify existing code, but others will try to 
rely predominantly on an AI agent.

## Setting up your skills environment

Skills in Peano are scattered all over the place and typically held next to the underlying
discussion. For example, the skill that creates all the documentation is held next to the 
documentation discussion in this folder. Most AI agents want all the skills together in their
own working directory. So when you start a new project, you first of all have to tell your system to 
become aware of all of these skills.

### Claude Code

Claude holds all the skills in a .skills directory. Therefore, please type in the following 
lines when you work with Claude code once per session:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Search all the directories of this project for SKILL.md files. If a directory holds a SKILL.md file, create a symlink to this directory in your local .claude/skills/.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

If this does not work, exit Claude and invoke the script documentation/load-skills.h. 


## Installing all the default skills

Peano employs very few default skills, but there are a few that we'd recommend to use:

1. The automatic source code generation, as it allows you to have a webbrowser open next to your
   skills terminal and read the outcomes as they drop in. In Claude Code, add this as post-hook:
   
   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
   /peano:regen-docs
   Invoke this skill every time after we alter a C++ or Python or documentation file in the background, i.e. make it a post-hook.
   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2. Ask the AI agent to always first present a plan before any changes are made.


<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
 Even though the documentation generation and some validation scripts might be automatic post-hooks, it makes sense to invoke them
 manually every now and then, and to read carefully through their details.
</div>


## Skill usage



## Create your own skills

With Claude code, we predominantly load the skill-creator which is loaded via

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
/anthropic-skills:skill-creator
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

And then tell them which file to write to explicitly. Once finished, you return the load skills command from above, and your are done.



# Python rationale and remarks

## Motivation

Previous generations of Peano and the first generation of ExaHyPE 2 relied on
C++ template meta programming to generate code and lacked any Python support.
In principle, this would have been a valid way forward, but we wanted to write a
piece of software which is accessible to colleagues without extensive C++
knowledge.

In the age of LLMs, we notably wanted to create a piece of software which is
easy to use with a language model. For this purpose, Python is hard to beat.


## Patterns and idioms

- In the original ExaHyPE 2 rewrite, we did rely a lot on Jinja 2 templates to
  generate all kind of files. As the code evolved, we decided that we want to
  move away from Jinja 2 templates as they are hard to maintain and to debug.
  Notably, they imply that knowledge is scattered between the template and the Python code,
  while we saw more and more logic migrate from the Python files into the templates.
- A Python layer only writes into the Python layer below it. Notably, no ExaHyPE 2 api generator writes directly into the Peano 4 layer. This is a design choice to avoid that knowledge is scattered across layers.
- @todo Avoid references via naming conventions. Insead use Python query.


# Hunting bugs

## Assertions

Before you write a single logDebug statement or chase a stack trace, rebuild against one
of the ```_debug``` or ```_asserts``` archive flavours (see the @ref sec_installation_build_variants "build variants" section of the installation page) and, ideally, link the ```_debug``` variant first. Release archives
compile assertions out entirely (```-DPeanoDebug=0```), so a bug that trips an invariant will
either silently corrupt state or segfault miles away from its actual cause. Assertions
are by far the fastest way to localise a defect - let the code tell you where it broke
before you start guessing.


## Logging

Peano's `tarch::logging` infrastructure (info, warning, error, debug and tracing
messages, plus log filters to narrow down which classes actually report) is documented
in full on the @ref tarch_logging "logging, tracing, statistics, profiling and assertions"
page. This section is not a repeat of that reference; it is the practical workflow to
follow when you actually have to hunt a bug:

1.  **Reproduce with the smallest configuration you can.** Fewer cells, one rank, one
    thread, a coarse mesh. Peano's traversal order is deterministic for a given setup, so
    a smaller reproducer keeps the same code paths without the noise of a production run.
2.  **Switch on debug logging for the suspect classes only**, rather than globally. A full
    `-DPeanoDebug=4` run on anything but a toy setup produces gigabytes of output you will
    never read. Use a log filter file (see the log filter section on the logging page) to
    whitelist just the classes/namespaces you suspect, e.g. the @ref glossary_observer "observer" or @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action set" you
    are debugging plus `peano4::grid` for the traversal itself.
3.  **Use `tarch::logging::LogFilter::switchProgramPhase()`** if the bug only shows up in
    one phase of a multi-step algorithm (e.g. only during grid construction, not during
    the time stepping). This lets you keep the filter file small and phase-specific instead
    of drowning in logs from unrelated phases.
    
## Study the FAQ

Peano offers @ref page_faq_home, which provide often sufficient information and tackle the
most common issues. After that, escalate the problem on Slack.

